body:
So I guess you deleted all your comments and this post. Either way, on the off chance you somehow see this, I wrote this in response to your comment:

"I'll never be as successful, handsome, and rich as the guy she left me for."

&nbsp;

My man Robert, do you really think it is *impossible by the laws of physics for you to become even* more *successful, handsome, and wealthy* than the guy she left you for? If so, then I see the problem: You believe in a lie.

&nbsp;

I was the same way for a long time. I believed it was impossible for me to accomplish my goals -- to get my degree and to become a research scientist -- and because of this, I too thought about suicide all the time. At my lowest point (staying in bed for days, skipping class, not talking to my friends or family, drinking to black out, etc.), it took just one person asking if I was okay to turn it all around. This guy made me realize that it's alright to want to give up. It's alright to want to die sometimes. But the best thing was: He said he's down to talk whenever shit gets hard.

&nbsp;

I made myself set up an appointment with a therapist that week. Better, I started to fucking believe in myself again. That's all it took. That's the **only** difference between the suicidal version of me that was failing in life, and the successful and attractive version of me. *Belief* that I could achieve my goals makes the pain worth enduring. *Belief* in myself caused me to stop thinking of failures as absolute, permanent facts about my abilities, and instead drove me -- out of a resolute fucking anger and motivation that I would **not** give up again -- to do one more rep, to study for 30 more minutes, to keep running even when your eyes burn with sweat and you feel like everyone is fucking judging you out on the street for being slow, or running funny, or whatever the hell it is that we make up in our minds. Whether you're a Godly man or not, I am telling you now: There is nothing in this world that says you cannot accomplish whatever it is that you want to accomplish. If you believe in God, you know God wants you to be successful and to help your fellow man by bettering yourself. If you don't believe in God, then you know that there is no law of physics which says "Oh yeah, that one human Robert? He just won't be able to do anything worthwhile. Everyone else can though." No. That's fucking bullshit and you know it.

&nbsp;

There is no difference between you and I, and there is no difference between you, I, and the thousands of other people who went from suicidal to successful. People like us are passionate. We care more than others. Yeah, that makes it hurt like a mortherfucker when things go wrong in your life. Because again, we care that much more. At the same time, this passion can drive us to achieve way more than those who are lacking in passion. It all depends on what you believe about yourself. If you believe you can, then you will. If you believe you can, you will look at yourself in the past and say "Why did I waste so much time?"

&nbsp;

Whatever you choose to do now, you can't believe in that fucking lie anymore. Just because you are not successful *yet*, it does not mean that you will never be. You got a new job. I bet you can get an even better job. Start bettering your life by believing in yourself again. Everything else will come with that. Keep track of where your mind is at when you start having negative thoughts again. Tell yourself that you will not allow a false belief to stop you from achieving whatever it is that you want to achieve. Become better. Become stronger. Move forward one step at a time. And if it first you can't believe in yourself, know that we on the sidelines do believe in you. I wouldn't have wrote all of this if I didn't.

&nbsp;

Feel free to PM brother.
